## The Bug

The issue is a **closure over the loop variable** `i`. In Go 1.21 (and earlier), the `for` loop variable `i` is a single variable whose value changes each iteration. All five goroutines close over that same variable. By the time any goroutine actually runs `fmt.Println(i)`, the loop has already completed and `i` holds its final value: **5**.

The problematic line is:

```go
go func() {
    defer wg.Done()
    fmt.Println(i)  // captures i by reference — all 5 goroutines share the same i
}()
```

## The Fix

Capture `i` by value at launch time. The idiomatic way is to pass it as a parameter to the anonymous function:

```go
go func(i int) {
    defer wg.Done()
    fmt.Println(i)
}(i)
```

<(solution language="go" verdict="fix">
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "sync"
)

func main() {
    var wg sync.WaitGroup
    for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
        wg.Add(1)
        go func(i int) {
            defer wg.Done()
            fmt.Println(i)
        }(i)
    }
    wg.Wait()
}
</solution>

